Understand the Goal and Constraints

Input: A string s of lowercase English letters.

Output: An integer representing the length of the longest substring.

Condition: Every character in the substring can appear at most 2 times.

Constraints: Length of s is up to 100. (Very small, meaning an O(N^2) or even O(N^3) approach would technically pass, but the goal is to show the optimal O(N) approach).

Initial Brainstorming (Brute Force)

Idea: Generate every possible substring using two nested loops, and then count the characters in each substring to see if it is valid.

Critique: Generating all substrings takes O(N^2) time. Checking each one takes O(N) time. Total time complexity would be O(N^3). Even if the counting is optimized, it is O(N^2). While this works for N=100, an interviewer will immediately ask: "Can you do better?"

Spotting the Pattern for Optimization

Observation: Imagine building a substring character by character from left to right. As soon as a character is added that pushes its frequency to 3, the substring is "broken" (invalid).

Key Insight: If the substring from index left to right is invalid, expanding it further to the right will never make it valid again. The only way to fix it is to shrink it from the left until the extra character is removed.

Algorithm Choice: This dynamic of expanding on one side and shrinking on the other perfectly matches the Sliding Window technique.

Formulating the Sliding Window Approach

Data Structure: Keep track of character frequencies in the current window. A dictionary (Hash Map) or an array of size 26 works perfectly. Let's call it char_count.

Pointers: Use a left pointer starting at 0, and a right pointer that iterates through the string.

The Logic Loop:

Expand the window by moving right and adding s[right] to char_count.

Check the condition: Is char_count[s[right]] > 2?

If yes, the window is invalid. Use a while loop to shrink the window from the left. Decrement the count of s[left] and increment left until char_count[s[right]] is back to 2.

Once the window is valid, calculate its length: right - left + 1.

Update max_length if this current valid window is the longest seen so far.

Dry Run (Mental Check with Example: "bcbb")

left = 0, right = 0. Window: "b". Counts: {b: 1}. Max: 1.

right = 1. Window: "bc". Counts: {b: 1, c: 1}. Max: 2.

right = 2. Window: "bcb". Counts: {b: 2, c: 1}. Max: 3.

right = 3. Window: "bcbb". Counts: {b: 3, c: 1}. Invalid!

Shrink: Remove s[left] ('b'). left becomes 1. Counts: {b: 2, c: 1}.

Window is now valid again: "cbb".

Check complete. The logic holds up perfectly.

Final Complexity Check

Time Complexity: O(N). Even though there is a while loop inside a for loop, both the left and right pointers only ever move forward. Each character is processed at most twice (once entering the window, once leaving).

Space Complexity: O(1). The char_count dictionary will hold at most 26 keys (since the problem guarantees only lowercase English letters). Because 26 is a constant upper bound, the space does not scale with the size of the input string.
